1. Set up your GitHub repository
Create a new repository. On GitHub, click the "+" icon in the top right corner and select New repository.
Choose a descriptive name. Pick a name that clearly identifies the project, for example, meta-spyder-search-engine.
Add a README.md file. Select the option to initialize your repository with a README.md. This file is your project's homepage and should contain a title, description, and instructions for setup.
Add a .gitignore file. Select the Python .gitignore template to automatically ignore common files like your virtual environment (venv) and compiled Python files.
Create the repository. Click Create repository to finish the process. 
2. Clone the repository and set up your project
Clone the repository. Open a terminal, navigate to your desired project directory, and clone the repository using the following command.

git clone https://github.com/your-username/meta-spyder-search-engine.git
cd meta-spyder-search-engine


Create a virtual environment. It's best practice to use a virtual environment to manage your project's dependencies.

python3 -m venv octoenv
source octoenv/bin/activate


Install project dependencies. Install all the Python libraries you need.

pip install fastapi scrapy elasticsearch podman-compose


Create the project structure. Organize your files and folders to keep your codebase clean and easy to navigate.
meta-spyder-search-engine/
├── app/                   # FastAPI web interface
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── main.py
├── web_crawler/           # Scrapy spiders
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── spiders/
│   └── scrapy.cfg
├── Dockerfile             # Defines your Docker images
├── docker-compose.yml     # Defines your multi-container application
├── requirements.txt       # Lists all Python dependencies
└── .gitignore             # Git ignore file
3. Build and containerize your application
Your docker-compose.yml file defines how your application's services run in containers. 

Modify your docker-compose.yml. As discussed in the previous turn, simplify your docker-compose.yml to remove the explicit dockerfile path.

yaml
# Simplified build instruction
services:
  web-interface:
    build: .
    # ... other options
  web-crawler:
    build: .
    # ... other options


Run with podman-compose. With the simplified configuration and your Podman permissions corrected, you can run your application.

podman-compose up -d


4. Use Git for version control
Use Git commands to track your progress and manage changes to your codebase.
Check the status. See which files have changed since your last commit.

git status


Add changes. Stage your changes for a commit.

git add .


Commit changes. Create a new commit with a descriptive message.

git commit -m "Initial project setup with FastAPI, Scrapy, and Elasticsearch"


Push to GitHub. Upload your changes to your remote GitHub repository.

git push origin main



